<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eluru</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eluru is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, in the delta region between the Godavari and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> rivers. The ancient city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a>, capital of the Salankayana dynasty and later of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-chalukyas">Eastern Chalukyas</a>, lay close by at the modern village of Pedavegi. Eluru is now a district headquarters and a centre of hand-knotted carpet weaving.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eluru.jpg" alt="Eluru" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: IM3847 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:District_Collectorate_of_West_Godavari_(May_2019)_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Eluru lies on the eastern coastal plains on the banks of the Tammileru River, about midway between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and Godavari rivers and some 50 kilometres inland from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>. The Tammileru flows through the city and empties into Kolleru Lake, a large shallow freshwater lake a few kilometres away that draws migratory birds between October and March. The Eluru Canal carries water from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-river">Krishna River</a> for irrigation. The city was historically known as Helapuri and Erla Uru before the British renamed it Ellore, and to avoid confusion with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vellore">Vellore</a> it was called Uppu Ellore, Salt Ellore, in the time of the Nizam.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The history of the area goes back to the second century CE. Vengi, near Eluru, was the capital of Andhra from the second century to the twelfth, ruled by the Salankayanas, the Vishnukudinas and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-chalukyas">Eastern Chalukyas</a>, and was capital of Andhradesa for longer than any other city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>.</p><p>The Salankayanas, feudatories of the Ikshvakus, ruled from the third to the fifth century, and an inscription of their first known king, Devavarman, records that he performed the Ashvamedha sacrifice to mark the founding of his kingdom. Eluru was later a prominent town under the Qutub Shahis in the 16th century and came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> in the 17th. In 1689 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> secured the renewal of its factory lease, including that for Eluru, after a dispute with the Mughals.</p><p>In 1894 farmers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> district held a conference in Eluru to protest against higher water rates. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> visited the town after the Congress meeting of 1921 and opened the Gandhi Andhra Jateeya Mahavidyalayam, a national college. The Eluru municipality was first constituted in 1866, the district hospital began as a dispensary around 1858, and Eluru became the headquarters of West Godavari district in 1925. A museum is planned for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plates of the 7th to 9th centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Eluru is the headquarters of Eluru district in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a> and a municipal corporation, and had a population of 214,414 at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Weaving of rugs has been a trade since the 17th century, and the city is known for its wool pile carpets. It is also a trade hub for fish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, oilseeds, tobacco and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>. In Eluru Buddha Park stands a 23-metre Buddha statue.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eluru” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
